 Today we're building a project on Python that will poll, with parallel pings (user defined number of threads), various IPs and make a compact tree-like structure show if they're up or down.
  The main program will have a web interface but will also poll, from time to time (user defined) the various hosts but will allow a user, from the web interface, to force a poll on request.
  This interface is publicly accessible but only logged in users will be able to force a poll.

  The various hosts will be stored in a database (in sqlite) with their Name, Grouping (where one or more hosts will be polled but their response will be considered as a single up or down
  state), Hierarchy (used to build the tree-like structure where this field will contain the name or ID of the parent host) and Location (a macro hierarchy that will be used to keep track of
  servers in different geographical locations). There will be an interface to allow the editing of this database, this will be part of a password protected part of the software only accessed
  by users with administrative permissions. This interface will allow for a CSV file to be used to initially load the hosts into the database.

  Another database (also in sqlite) will store at least 24h of history per host (user-defined upper limit) and, when clicked on in the main tree-like structure, it will show a timelime of
  up/down states of the select host. In the main tree-like structure there will be an abbreviated graph showing the up/down stats of the past half hour.

  The software will be optimized and as secure as possible to save resources but allow an expanding database.

  The code will be properly formated and all variables (such as auto-polling time, TCP port and other information deemed important) will be able to be changed within the administrative
  interface or through an external .cfg file.

  The interface should be visually simple to allow at-a-glance information of up/down states acroos the whole network and/or Locations. Consider that the site should be optimized for
  widescreen monitors (720p or 1080p) so a left-to-right format should be prefered to a top-down format.

